.TH BIGFLOATNORMALIZE 3 "2026-09-22" "BigNums" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
bigFloatNormalize, bigFloatTruncate \- normalize or reduce a big float mantissa
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqbignums.h\(dq
.sp
.BI "int bigFloatNormalize(BigFloat *" "x" ");"
.sp
.BI "void bigFloatTruncate(BigFloat *" "x" ", int " "target_limbs" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bigFloatNormalize()
removes leading zero limbs, then left-shifts the mantissa until the most
significant limb has bit 31 set.  It reduces exp by the shift count to
preserve the value.  A zero mantissa becomes canonical positive zero.
Normalization supports mantissas using all MAX_LIMBS (256) limbs.
.PP
.B bigFloatTruncate()
clamps target_limbs to the range 1 through MAX_LIMBS (256).
If the mantissa is larger than the target,
it discards low whole limbs, increases exp by 32 times the discarded limb
count, and normalizes.  Otherwise it does nothing.
Truncation loses low bits; it does not round to nearest or preserve the exact
value.  This is a public helper.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Normalization returns 0 or INT_MAX if the normalized exponent cannot be
represented.  Errors leave x unchanged.
Truncation returns no value; on exponent overflow it sets errno to ERANGE
and leaves x unchanged.  Include <errno.h> to inspect this error.
Set errno to zero before calling truncation to distinguish a new range error
from a previously stored value; success does not clear errno.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bigFloatReciprocal (3),
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
